{#
  Filmstrip, a drag-along band of site photography.

  A full-bleed row of identical, near-full-height frames on the dark ground,
  dragged sideways with the mouse or swiped on touch. No heading, no captions:
  it is a way to show the work, and it carries on from whatever section sits
  above it. Let go mid-drag and the nearest frame settles back into the centre.

  Behaviour (progressive enhancement): the track is a centre-snapping
  horizontal scroll list that works with no JS at all. filmstrip.js adds the
  mouse drag, the fling-and-settle, the "Drag" cursor cue and keyboard control
  on top.

  Expects `strip` — an array of { image, alt }. Set it on the page before
  including, the way index.njk sets `steps`. Six to eight frames is the sweet
  spot: enough that the row never looks short, few enough to reach the end.

  Every frame is cropped to one fixed box, so photographs of different shapes
  can be dropped in without breaking the rhythm. Landscape sources suit it
  best, since that is the shape being cropped to.
#}
{% if strip and strip.length %}
<section class="filmstrip" aria-label="{{ filmstripLabel or 'Photographs from our work' }}">
  <ul class="filmstrip__track" role="list">
    {% for f in strip %}
    <li class="filmstrip__item">
      <img src="{{ f.image }}" alt="{{ f.alt }}" width="1200" height="961" loading="lazy" decoding="async" draggable="false">
    </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <div class="filmstrip__cue" aria-hidden="true"><span>Drag</span></div>
</section>
{% endif %}
